WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:13.470
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to our table today as we open Reza Shah Kazemi's spiritual ethos to uncover the Imam's teaching on the remembrance of God and to deeply examine why the very simplest act of worship is designated as the absolute greatest.

2
00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:20.510
<v Speaker 1>God has made the remembrance a polish for the hearts by which they hear after being deaf and see after being blind.

3
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.670
<v Speaker 2>It is such a profound starting point.

4
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.550
<v Speaker 1>It really is.

5
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:24.670
<v Speaker 1>And

6
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:37.470
<v Speaker 1>Our mission in this deep dive is to trace the contours of what tikr truly means, to examine the foundations of why it outranks every other right, and to uncover how exactly a polished heart comes to see God.

7
00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.910
<v Speaker 2>Well, we have a lot of ground to cover.

8
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:45.950
<v Speaker 1>We do, but I mean we actually have to begin by confronting A profound paradox that is planted at the very center of Islamic spirituality.

9
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.830
<v Speaker 1>I want you to consider the sheer physical endurance demanded by the traditional pillars of worship.

10
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.750
<v Speaker 1>Like, think of the fasting month.

11
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:53.310
<v Speaker 1>Oh, sure.

12
00:00:53.630 --> 00:01:04.910
<v Speaker 1>The believer abstains from food and water from dawn until dusk, which historically and even today in many parts of the world means enduring scorching heat, intense physical fatigue, and just the deep visceral ache of hunger.

13
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.510
<v Speaker 2>It is an undeniable, grueling physical tool.

14
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.070
<v Speaker 1>It is.

15
00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.230
<v Speaker 1>And then consider almsgiving, which requires the parting of hard-earned wealth.

16
00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.590
<v Speaker 1>I mean, this cuts directly into our deepest human attachments to security and comfort.

17
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.750
<v Speaker 2>And just the basic anxiety of survival, Precisely.

18
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.230
<v Speaker 1>You are quite literally giving away your earthly security.

19
00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.870
<v Speaker 2>It is a profound material sacrifice.

20
00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:28.270
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

21
00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.670
<v Speaker 1>And then, consider the pilgrimage.

22
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:36.110
<v Speaker 1>Historically, this demanded A treacherous months-long journey across unforgiving deserts.

23
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.430
<v Speaker 2>An undertaking completely fraught with peril, bandits, disease.

24
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.710
<v Speaker 1>Immense financial expense.

25
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.710
<v Speaker 1>People would actually write their wills before they left because survival was not guaranteed.

26
00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.430
<v Speaker 2>I see.

27
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.750
<v Speaker 1>Even the fixed daily prayers demand specific times that interrupt sleep and commerce.

28
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:58.030
<v Speaker 1>They require rigid postures and strict ritual purity, sometimes involving washing with freezing water in the depths of winter.

29
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:04.230
<v Speaker 1>Every single one of these pillars commands a heavy, undeniable toll in time, wealth, or physical exertion.

30
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:16.510
<v Speaker 1>Yet the tradition singles out an act that costs no money, requires no journey, demands no physical exhaustion, and can be performed while simply walking through a marketplace, sitting in a chair or eating a meal.

31
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:23.230
<v Speaker 1>The bare silent turning of the heart and tongue toward the Creator is called the greatest of all works.

32
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.470
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

33
00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.150
<v Speaker 2>How can the easiest act of worship outrank fasting, charity, and battle?

34
00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.030
<v Speaker 2>I mean, it seems entirely counterintuitive to human nature.

35
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.670
<v Speaker 2>We operate on an economy of effort, you know.

36
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:35.070
<v Speaker 1>We do.

37
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:41.950
<v Speaker 2>We just assume that the value of an action is strictly proportional to the suffering or the effort required to execute it.

38
00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.550
<v Speaker 2>in our physical lives, in our careers, in our societies.

39
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.510
<v Speaker 2>We believe that if you do not sweat, you do not earn the prize.

40
00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:54.670
<v Speaker 2>If I exhaust my body in fasting or give away half my life savings, the tangible sacrifice is undeniable.

41
00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:04.510
<v Speaker 2>To claim that simply turning the mind or the tongue toward God supersedes those immense physical, material, and mortal sacrifices, it requires A monumental justification.

42
00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:06.350
<v Speaker 2>It sounds almost too convenient.

43
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.510
<v Speaker 1>That friction is entirely necessary.

44
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:14.430
<v Speaker 1>I mean, we are conditioned from childhood to measure value by the sweat on our brow or the ache in our muscles.

45
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:14.870
<v Speaker 2>Definitely.

46
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.390
<v Speaker 1>If it does not hurt, we just assume it is not working.

47
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.310
<v Speaker 1>But to resolve this paradox, we must journey inward.

48
00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:31.470
<v Speaker 1>We must move past the outward societal consequences of worship to uncover what remembrance actually does to the hidden microscopic depths of a human being.

49
00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.590
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I'm listening.

50
00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.070
<v Speaker 1>Fasting starves the physical body.

51
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.150
<v Speaker 1>Almsgiving starves the ego's greed.

52
00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:43.430
<v Speaker 1>But these actions, noble and foundational as they are, act upon the perimeter of the human condition.

53
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.590
<v Speaker 2>They manage the borders.

54
00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:45.230
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

55
00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.830
<v Speaker 1>We are searching for the root.

56
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.750
<v Speaker 1>We are looking for the practice that acts directly upon the center of consciousness itself.

57
00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.870
<v Speaker 2>So where do we find that center?

58
00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.750
<v Speaker 1>To find it, we must look to the source of this teaching.

59
00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.350
<v Speaker 1>We turn to the Imam, peace be upon him.

60
00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.910
<v Speaker 1>He stands at the head of the entire mystical tradition.

61
00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:07.230
<v Speaker 1>Throughout history, he is recognized as the gate to the city of prophetic wisdom.

62
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:12.030
<v Speaker 1>His presence marks every noble expression of the spirit within this faith.

63
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:21.390
<v Speaker 1>Even the ancient craft guilds, the leather workers, the masons, the blacksmiths, they trace their codes of spiritual chivalry back to his teachings.

64
00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:28.350
<v Speaker 2>His influence really spans from the grit of the marketplace all the way to the highest, most refined expressions of poetry and art.

65
00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.230
<v Speaker 1>It does.

66
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.750
<v Speaker 1>His teachings form an organic, load-bearing bridge.

67
00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.190
<v Speaker 1>He takes the early scriptural verses regarding remembrance

68
00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:42.590
<v Speaker 1>and distills them, foreshadowing the elaborate complex doctrines that later mystics would spend centuries constructing.

69
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.630
<v Speaker 1>And from this point forward, we will simply refer to him as the Imam.

70
00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:46.190
<v Speaker 2>I see.

71
00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.750
<v Speaker 2>And his position as a unifying figure is truly singular in the history of religion.

72
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:51.310
<v Speaker 1>Very much so.

73
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:57.870
<v Speaker 2>When we examine the historical development of these spiritual paths, we see a tradition that is profoundly shared and reverent.

74
00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.950
<v Speaker 2>I mean, the Imam unites what is often perceived as the esoteric core of the religion.

75
00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.190
<v Speaker 2>This core expresses itself distinctly through Sunni Sufism and Sheet

76
00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.710
<v Speaker 2>Two streams that history sometimes paints as divergent.

77
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.230
<v Speaker 1>But they aren't, really.

78
00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:16.910
<v Speaker 2>No, because the great unifier taught a principle that completely collapses those historical boundaries.

79
00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:26.510
<v Speaker 2>He argued that true followers of the imams are inherently walking the mystical path, and concurrently, true mystics are deeply rooted in the teachings of the imams.

80
00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:35.230
<v Speaker 1>The outward historical differences, the sectarian lines drawn by politicians and dynasties, are entirely dissolved in the fire of sanctity and gnosis.

81
00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.070
<v Speaker 2>That dissolution is vital.

82
00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.310
<v Speaker 2>We are not looking at a fractured philosophy.

83
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.830
<v Speaker 2>We are looking at a shared, unified human inheritance.

84
00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:44.030
<v Speaker 1>We are.

85
00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:49.230
<v Speaker 1>But to grasp the magnitude of this inheritance, we must define the instrument itself.

86
00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:55.950
<v Speaker 1>We need to establish what remembrance actually means in this context, because our modern vocabulary really betrays us here.

87
00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:56.230
<v Speaker 2>How so?

88
00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:00.430
<v Speaker 1>Well, the lexicographer offers a definition that cuts through our modern misconceptions.

89
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.150
<v Speaker 1>He writes that remembrance is not merely memory.

90
00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:06.670
<v Speaker 1>It is that by which a person keeps present the knowledge they have attained.

91
00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:07.310
<v Speaker 2>Oh, I see.

92
00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.870
<v Speaker 1>This is the critical hinge of our entire discussion.

93
00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:15.950
<v Speaker 1>It is not the act of storing a fact away in the brain, like filing a document in a dusty cabinet to be retrieved later.

94
00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:26.430
<v Speaker 1>It is the active, intense, relentless process of rendering a known reality vividly present to consciousness here and now.

95
00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:31.550
<v Speaker 2>The distinction between passive memory and rendering something present is massive.

96
00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.550
<v Speaker 2>I mean, memory as we typically experience it is entirely passive and retrospective.

97
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:35.790
<v Speaker 2>True.

98
00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:42.030
<v Speaker 2>You recall a childhood home or the date of a historical battle or just where you parked your car.

99
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.830
<v Speaker 2>The information is retrieved from cold storage, observed briefly by the mine and then put back.

100
00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.190
<v Speaker 2>It has no bearing on your immediate state of being.

101
00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:49.710
<v Speaker 1>Definitely not.

102
00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:53.630
<v Speaker 2>But the lexicographer is describing an active burning presencing.

103
00:06:54.110 --> 00:07:04.230
<v Speaker 2>It is the difference between knowing intellectually that the sun is a star millions of miles away and standing directly in a desert at noon, blinded by its light and feeling its heat blister your skin.

104
00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.550
<v Speaker 1>Now that is a perfect way to put it.

105
00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:09.150
<v Speaker 1>You are not retrieving A theological concept of a distant creator.

106
00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:15.110
<v Speaker 1>You are pulling the reality of the divine into the immediate overwhelming foreground of your awareness.

107
00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:18.110
<v Speaker 1>Furthermore, possesses this fascinating dual nature.

108
00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.510
<v Speaker 1>It is simultaneously the end and the means.

109
00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:21.470
<v Speaker 2>The end and the means.

110
00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:22.110
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

111
00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:27.950
<v Speaker 1>It is the ultimate spiritual awareness itself, the final destination of the seeker.

112
00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:34.110
<v Speaker 1>And it is the methodic, rigorous discipline used to cultivate that very awareness.

113
00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.630
<v Speaker 1>It is the hammer, the anvil, and the sword all at once.

114
00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:41.070
<v Speaker 1>You invoke the name to achieve the state of invocation.

115
00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.110
<v Speaker 2>That dual nature explains a great deal about why it permeates the tradition so thoroughly.

116
00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:50.750
<v Speaker 2>You practice the act of presencing to achieve a state of continuous presence.

117
00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:51.950
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

118
00:07:52.190 --> 00:08:00.270
<v Speaker 2>But if this practice is the core, if it is the ultimate foundation, how does it escape the boundaries that confine all those other rituals we discussed earlier?

119
00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:04.030
<v Speaker 1>Well, it escapes those boundaries precisely because it is commanded without a ceiling.

120
00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.030
<v Speaker 2>Without a ceiling, okay.

121
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:06.670
<v Speaker 1>Look at the architecture of the other rites.

122
00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.350
<v Speaker 1>We exist in a world that loves boundaries and checklists.

123
00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.350
<v Speaker 1>The daily prayers are strictly numbered.

124
00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.030
<v Speaker 1>There are 5, not 50.

125
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.350
<v Speaker 1>Fasting is bounded by the dawn and dusk of a single specific month.

126
00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:21.550
<v Speaker 2>Pilgrimage is required, ideally just once in a lifetime.

127
00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.310
<v Speaker 1>These are finite obligations.

128
00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.030
<v Speaker 1>You can complete them, check the box, and say, I have done my duty.

129
00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:29.870
<v Speaker 1>Remembrance alone is commanded in the imperative without any limitation.

130
00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:33.950
<v Speaker 1>The scripture tells the believer to remember with much remembrance.

131
00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:37.630
<v Speaker 1>It is the singular work you are told never to have done enough of.

132
00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.310
<v Speaker 1>There is no finish line.

133
00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:40.670
<v Speaker 1>There is no quota.

134
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:44.270
<v Speaker 2>If this demands A perpetual state of presence and deep retreat,

135
00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:48.750
<v Speaker 2>Isn't that just an escape from the demands and duties of the real world?

136
00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:49.470
<v Speaker 1>How do you mean?

137
00:08:49.710 --> 00:09:01.070
<v Speaker 2>Well, it paints a picture of a practitioner locked away in a dark room, severing all ties with society, family, and civic duty solely to maintain this unbroken stream of consciousness.

138
00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.990
<v Speaker 2>It sounds like an invasion of the human condition rather than an elevation of it.

139
00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:05.710
<v Speaker 1>I see.

140
00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:11.790
<v Speaker 2>If the greatest act of worship requires me to ignore my neighbors and abandon my responsibilities, that seems deeply flawed.

141
00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:14.630
<v Speaker 1>I understand the concern and it is actually.

142
00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:19.390
<v Speaker 1>a common critique of mystical traditions, but the tradition anticipates it perfectly.

143
00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:24.670
<v Speaker 1>The retreat, as noted by the modern scholar of Nostros, is indeed a method.

144
00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.710
<v Speaker 1>It is a temporary isolation required to build capacity.

145
00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:28.750
<v Speaker 2>Like a training ground.

146
00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:37.230
<v Speaker 1>Just like that, you retreat to the quiet room to forge the discipline, to learn the heavy lifting of the mind without the distraction of the world pulling at your senses.

147
00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.430
<v Speaker 1>But the ultimate goal is not to stay in that room.

148
00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:48.990
<v Speaker 1>The great reviver theologian highlights that the true aim is a continuous presence of heart that dignifies and strengthens every other act in the world.

149
00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:54.750
<v Speaker 1>The goal is to carry that intense inward silence into the loud, chaotic marketplace.

150
00:09:55.150 --> 00:10:03.870
<v Speaker 1>The goal is to strike the anvil, trade the goods, sign the contracts, and feed the poor, all while the heart remains anchored in the uninterrupted presence of the real.

151
00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:05.470
<v Speaker 1>It does not negate the world.

152
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.710
<v Speaker 1>It transforms the mundane world into a theater of worship.

153
00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:10.350
<v Speaker 2>I see.

154
00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:14.510
<v Speaker 2>The integration into daily life elevates the concept entirely.

155
00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:18.830
<v Speaker 2>It ceases to be an act of monastic avoidance and becomes an act of radical engagement.

156
00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:19.550
<v Speaker 1>It truly does.

157
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:26.910
<v Speaker 2>You are engaging with the material world, dealing with difficult people, managing crises, but you are operating from a fortified, luminous center.

158
00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.870
<v Speaker 2>You are no longer swept away by the current of daily anxieties because you are tethered to something immovable.

159
00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:35.990
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to the verdict of the Prophet, peace and blessings of a lobby upon

160
00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.150
<v Speaker 1>him and his family.

161
00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:40.030
<v Speaker 1>And from here, we will just refer to him as the prophet.

162
00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:40.590
<v Speaker 2>Okay.

163
00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:46.910
<v Speaker 1>He posed a profound question to his companions, asking if he should tell them of the best and purest of their works.

164
00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:55.550
<v Speaker 1>He stated it is more exalted than giving away mountains of gold and silver, better even than meeting the enemy in battle and striking their necks or having one's own neck struck.

165
00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:56.430
<v Speaker 2>My goodness.

166
00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.790
<v Speaker 1>And the answer he gave them was the perpetual invocation of God.

167
00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:05.870
<v Speaker 1>To emphasize the absolute gravity of this, he offered a stark, terrifying image.

168
00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:12.590
<v Speaker 1>He said the likeness of the one who remembers their Lord and the one who does not remember him is the likeness of the living and the dead.

169
00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:13.710
<v Speaker 2>The living and the dead.

170
00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.550
<v Speaker 1>We must dwell deeply on this reality.

171
00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:17.870
<v Speaker 1>To be heedless is not just to be...

172
00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.790
<v Speaker 1>a negligent practitioner or a slightly flawed human being.

173
00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:24.110
<v Speaker 1>It is to be a corpse that walks.

174
00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:26.270
<v Speaker 1>The physical body may be exceptionally busy.

175
00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:34.590
<v Speaker 1>It may be accumulating vast wealth, fighting literal or corporate battles, building massive monuments, or just endlessly scrolling through meaningless noise.

176
00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:38.110
<v Speaker 1>But the spiritual pulse has completely stopped.

177
00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.390
<v Speaker 1>The inner life is entirely extinct.

178
00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:41.230
<v Speaker 2>The walking dead.

179
00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.150
<v Speaker 2>It is a chilling extrapolation.

180
00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.510
<v Speaker 2>We define life biologically by a heartbeat, by respiration, by brainwave activity.

181
00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.790
<v Speaker 2>If those metrics are present, we declare a person alive.

182
00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:52.030
<v Speaker 1>Sure.

183
00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:58.130
<v Speaker 2>This analogy demands a spiritual definition of life that makes our biological metrics look superficial.

184
00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:08.250
<v Speaker 2>I mean, a person can have a perfectly functioning cardiovascular system, a sharp intellect, and a vibrant social life, yet be entirely deceased in the realm that actually matters.

185
00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:09.330
<v Speaker 1>Completely deceased.

186
00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.370
<v Speaker 2>The body is a functioning machine, but the occupant is gone.

187
00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:16.610
<v Speaker 2>The heedless person interacts with the world merely as a collision of matter.

188
00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:22.450
<v Speaker 2>They see a tree as timber, they see a person as a resource or an obstacle, completely blind to the underlying

189
00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.530
<v Speaker 2>that sustains their very existence.

190
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:29.170
<v Speaker 2>They are sleepwalking through a miraculous existence, confusing the dream for reality.

191
00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:33.810
<v Speaker 1>And the ultimate tragedy of the walking corpse is that it does not know it is dead.

192
00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.530
<v Speaker 1>It feels the warmth of the sun on its skin.

193
00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.050
<v Speaker 1>It tastes sweet food.

194
00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:41.890
<v Speaker 1>It feels the rush of adrenaline, and it thinks it is fully alive.

195
00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:43.690
<v Speaker 2>It has no idea what it's missing.

196
00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:49.010
<v Speaker 1>It is entirely ignorant of the greater light, the profound oceanic reality it is missing.

197
00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:54.850
<v Speaker 1>It is like a person born in a lightless cave who thinks the damp walls are the extent of the universe.

198
00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:58.690
<v Speaker 1>This establishes the dire necessity of the spiritual pulse.

199
00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.130
<v Speaker 1>But we must understand the mechanism of revival.

200
00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:04.130
<v Speaker 1>How does a corpse wake up?

201
00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.770
<v Speaker 1>How does a blinded organ learn to see again?

202
00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:13.570
<v Speaker 1>God has made the remembrance a polish for the hearts by which they hear after being deaf and see after being blind.

203
00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.490
<v Speaker 2>The rusted mirror burnish clean.

204
00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.290
<v Speaker 2>This provides the physical mechanism of the practice.

205
00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:18.850
<v Speaker 1>It does.

206
00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:25.730
<v Speaker 1>The heart in this tradition is not the blood pumping muscle in the chest, nor is it the seat of mere fluctuating emotion.

207
00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.770
<v Speaker 1>It is an organ of deep, profound perception.

208
00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:34.370
<v Speaker 1>It is the spiritual center of the human being, the cognitive core where intellect and spirit merge.

209
00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:34.650
<v Speaker 2>I see.

210
00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:38.530
<v Speaker 1>Currently for The Walking Dead, this organ is thickly clouded by rust.

211
00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:40.450
<v Speaker 1>The rust is forgetfulness.

212
00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:46.290
<v Speaker 1>It is the accumulation of daily distractions, the unchecked ego, the minor and major sins,

213
00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:54.130
<v Speaker 1>Layer by layer, day by day, this oxidation builds up until the mirror is completely opaque.

214
00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:58.370
<v Speaker 1>Remembrance is the rigorous, abrasive rubbing away of that rust.

215
00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:03.730
<v Speaker 1>Let us dissect the etymology of the word polish as used by the imam in this context.

216
00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:09.250
<v Speaker 1>The root word implies a becoming clear and exposing to view, a bringing into the open.

217
00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:14.450
<v Speaker 1>It is the same linguistic root used to describe the break of day when it finally brings the sun to light.

218
00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:16.010
<v Speaker 1>banishing the darkness.

219
00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:18.050
<v Speaker 2>So you are not painting a new picture on the heart.

220
00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:18.290
<v Speaker 1>No.

221
00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:21.330
<v Speaker 1>You are not acquiring some foreign alien knowledge.

222
00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:26.690
<v Speaker 1>You are furiously, diligently scraping away the accumulated grime to reveal what is already there.

223
00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:29.690
<v Speaker 2>The physical effort implied by polishing is incredibly telling.

224
00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:34.290
<v Speaker 2>I mean, burnishing a deeply rusted sheet of metal is not a gentle, passive act.

225
00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:35.090
<v Speaker 1>It requires friction.

226
00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.970
<v Speaker 2>It requires consistent, repetitive force.

227
00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:43.170
<v Speaker 2>If you just lightly brush a rusted mirror with a soft cloth, nothing happens.

228
00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:49.090
<v Speaker 2>The practice of invocation, the endless repetition, is the friction against the rust.

229
00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:51.010
<v Speaker 2>It can be exhausting.

230
00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:55.090
<v Speaker 2>It forces the practitioner to confront the ugliness of their own distractions.

231
00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:58.930
<v Speaker 2>And the dual action of this mirror is profoundly beautiful.

232
00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:04.290
<v Speaker 2>On one side, the polished surface is cleared to reflect the truth coming down from above.

233
00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.650
<v Speaker 1>But it is not just a piece of passive glass waiting for an external beam.

234
00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:08.450
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

235
00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.290
<v Speaker 2>I mean, definitely not.

236
00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:17.090
<v Speaker 2>The Persian poet offers imamate of breasts polished by invocation to receive the pure, unspotted image.

237
00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:23.370
<v Speaker 2>The clearing of the surface simultaneously uncovers buried treasures of the intellect latent within the heart itself.

238
00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:26.130
<v Speaker 1>The heart is remembering something it always inherently knew.

239
00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:30.290
<v Speaker 1>It possesses an ingrained primordial knowledge of the Creator.

240
00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.050
<v Speaker 2>The rust merely obscured this internal incandescence.

241
00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:33.330
<v Speaker 1>Correct.

242
00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:38.850
<v Speaker 1>The polish clears the window to the sky, yes, but it also excavates the luminous jewels buried in the floor of the soul.

243
00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:41.570
<v Speaker 1>But this leads us to the ultimate question of perception.

244
00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:47.330
<v Speaker 1>If the heart undergoes this rigorous burnishing, if the rust is cleared away through decades of

245
00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:51.410
<v Speaker 1>and the organ recovers its sight, what does it actually see?

246
00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:55.570
<v Speaker 1>The Imam provides an answer of breathtaking audacity.

247
00:15:56.050 --> 00:16:00.210
<v Speaker 1>When asked if he had seen his Lord, he states that the heart sees God.

248
00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.050
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay, if the heart sees God.

249
00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.090
<v Speaker 2>Isn't that just a feeling dressed up as sight?

250
00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:06.530
<v Speaker 1>I understand why you'd say that.

251
00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:10.210
<v Speaker 2>We use the word see metaphorically all the time in everyday language.

252
00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.970
<v Speaker 2>We say, I see what you mean to indicate comprehension.

253
00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:15.970
<v Speaker 2>We say, I see the logic in your argument.

254
00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:16.450
<v Speaker 1>We do.

255
00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:30.450
<v Speaker 2>To claim the heart literally sees the divine feels like taking a subjective emotional swell, an overwhelming feeling of peace or awe during meditation and dressing it up in the vocabulary of optical vision to make it sound more authoritative.

256
00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:33.330
<v Speaker 2>It sounds like poetry masquerading as literal truth.

257
00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:40.690
<v Speaker 1>It is entirely necessary to challenge that claim because without a massive epistemological shift, the claim collapses into absurdity.

258
00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:43.210
<v Speaker 1>We must avoid a fundamental category mistake.

259
00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:44.210
<v Speaker 2>Okay, lay it out for me.

260
00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:48.130
<v Speaker 1>The physical eye is strictly bound to the surfaces of the sensible world.

261
00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:53.090
<v Speaker 1>It is a biological instrument that registers photons bouncing off physical matter.

262
00:16:53.250 --> 00:17:01.250
<v Speaker 1>It cannot see justice, it cannot see love, it cannot see gravity, and it certainly cannot see the limitless non-material divine reality.

263
00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:06.210
<v Speaker 1>To expect the physical eye to see God is a flaw in basic logic.

264
00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:09.650
<v Speaker 1>It is like trying to measure the temperature of a room with a ruler.

265
00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:10.130
<v Speaker 2>I see.

266
00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.010
<v Speaker 1>The heart's sight is not empirical observation.

267
00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.250
<v Speaker 1>It does not measure wavelength or distance.

268
00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:17.330
<v Speaker 1>It sees through the verities of faith.

269
00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:19.810
<v Speaker 1>It is not the acquisition of a new data point.

270
00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:21.890
<v Speaker 1>It is the presencing of a reality.

271
00:17:22.330 --> 00:17:27.410
<v Speaker 1>When the heart is polished, it perceives the underlying truth that sustains all physical matter.

272
00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:28.770
<v Speaker 1>It perceives the real.

273
00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.010
<v Speaker 2>I follow the epistemological distinction.

274
00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:35.330
<v Speaker 2>We are talking about an organ designed to perceive a different dimension of reality entirely.

275
00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:39.730
<v Speaker 2>Just as the ear cannot perceive light, the physical eye cannot perceive the divine.

276
00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:40.370
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

277
00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:45.010
<v Speaker 2>The spiritual heart is the specific receptor tuned to this frequency of existence.

278
00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:48.370
<v Speaker 1>And this brings us to a beautiful convergence.

279
00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:53.650
<v Speaker 1>There is a Quranic imagery the Imam was referencing when discussing the polish.

280
00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:54.850
<v Speaker 2>Light upon light.

281
00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:55.250
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

282
00:17:55.610 --> 00:18:00.610
<v Speaker 1>Imagine a lamp situated in a niche, enclosed in glass, lit from a blessed tree.

283
00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:08.850
<v Speaker 1>When all art is burnished, the latent inner light of the polished heart, the inherent primordial knowledge we discussed, rises up.

284
00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:12.690
<v Speaker 1>It ascends to meet the overarching descending light from on high.

285
00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:17.810
<v Speaker 1>The internal illumination and the external revelation meet and recognize each other.

286
00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:19.490
<v Speaker 1>It is light upon light.

287
00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.410
<v Speaker 1>The seer and the scene are illuminated by the same singular radiance.

288
00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:25.490
<v Speaker 2>The geometry of that image is striking.

289
00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:26.130
<v Speaker 1>Isn't it?

290
00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:30.530
<v Speaker 2>The niche contains and focuses the light, preventing it from dissipating into the void.

291
00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.610
<v Speaker 2>The polished heart becomes the niche.

292
00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:40.610
<v Speaker 2>It holds the latent light, tending it through disciplined remembrance, protecting it from the winds of distraction until it is pure enough to merge with the descending light.

293
00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:45.410
<v Speaker 1>But the paradox deepens still, and we must venture into the most profound territory of this teaching.

294
00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.890
<v Speaker 1>We turn to the concept explored by the early Sufi

295
00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:52.370
<v Speaker 1>The tradition teaches a stunning reality about the culmination of this path.

296
00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:53.170
<v Speaker 2>What is that?

297
00:18:53.410 --> 00:19:01.490
<v Speaker 1>Through intense, unbroken devotion, God becomes the very hearing by which the servant hears and the sight by which the servant sees.

298
00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:07.490
<v Speaker 1>If the divine enters into the outward acts of perception in this way, we face a staggering conclusion.

299
00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:14.170
<v Speaker 1>The supreme act of spiritual awareness, this ultimate vision of the heart, is not actually man's vision of God.

300
00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:19.090
<v Speaker 1>It is God's vision of God, affected through the purified space of the human heart.

301
00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.850
<v Speaker 2>Oh my goodness, I completely concede the magnitude of that.

302
00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:23.010
<v Speaker 1>It is a lot to take in.

303
00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.530
<v Speaker 2>It completely dismantles our standard framework of knowing.

304
00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:31.530
<v Speaker 2>I had viewed the seeker as the subject, looking outward at God as the object, like an astronomer

305
00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.490
<v Speaker 2>are looking at a star through a telescope.

306
00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:38.210
<v Speaker 2>But if God becomes the site itself, the dichotomy completely collapses.

307
00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:38.770
<v Speaker 1>It does.

308
00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:42.850
<v Speaker 2>I grasp the immensity of the Imam's command to know God through God.

309
00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:48.330
<v Speaker 2>You cannot build knowledge of the divine from below, stacking empirical facts like bricks to reach heaven.

310
00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:49.010
<v Speaker 1>No, you cannot.

311
00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:54.930
<v Speaker 2>True knowledge is the real coming to know itself within the cleared, polished space of the human subject.

312
00:19:55.490 --> 00:20:01.970
<v Speaker 2>The human being provides the burnished mirror, but the light that sees and the light that is seen are one and the same.

313
00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:05.890
<v Speaker 1>The concession is profound because it requires the ultimate surrender.

314
00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.130
<v Speaker 1>It is the surrender of the ego as the primary agent of knowledge.

315
00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:18.770
<v Speaker 1>The eye that demands to see God, the eye that wants to claim this spiritual trophy of enlightenment, must fade entirely, leaving only the polished mirror.

316
00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:24.530
<v Speaker 1>Now, we must ground this highly elevated, dizzying state back into the physical world you inhabit.

317
00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:31.090
<v Speaker 1>What does the person with the polished heart actually experience when they walk out of the retreat and into the blinding sun of the modern marketplace?

318
00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.930
<v Speaker 2>That is the ultimate test, isn't it?

319
00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:33.090
<v Speaker 1>Is.

320
00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:36.050
<v Speaker 1>The objective reality is that the purified heart sees the divine

321
00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:37.890
<v Speaker 1>attributes manifested everywhere.

322
00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.930
<v Speaker 1>The tree, the stone, the stranger's face, the falling rain.

323
00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:44.370
<v Speaker 1>Nothing that exists can be shut out of the divine reality.

324
00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:49.410
<v Speaker 1>Every single atom is recognized as a locus of manifestation for the real.

325
00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:53.970
<v Speaker 2>It transforms the mundane world into an unbroken landscape of revelation.

326
00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:58.130
<v Speaker 2>Every interaction, every glance becomes a reading of a divine text.

327
00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:01.450
<v Speaker 2>But there is a sheer theological cliff edge here.

328
00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:06.050
<v Speaker 2>I mean, if you see the divine in the tree or the stone, you risk sliding

329
00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:07.410
<v Speaker 2>to a simple pantheism.

330
00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:15.290
<v Speaker 2>You risk collapsing the creator into the created, treating the universe itself as God, which violates the core of the monotheistic vision.

331
00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:19.090
<v Speaker 1>Which is why the imam provides a critical, unbreakable safeguard.

332
00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:23.010
<v Speaker 1>He states that God is with everything, but not through association.

333
00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:26.130
<v Speaker 1>And he is other than everything, but not through separation.

334
00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:26.610
<v Speaker 2>I see.

335
00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:31.250
<v Speaker 1>This is the delicate, magnificent tension of imminence and transcendence.

336
00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:39.570
<v Speaker 1>He is closer to the human being than the jugular vein, permeating reality, yet he remains utterly incomparable and beyond all created things.

337
00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:44.130
<v Speaker 1>The polished heart holds these two truths in perfect unyielding balance.

338
00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:47.170
<v Speaker 2>It is the ultimate cognitive and spiritual endurance test.

339
00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:58.850
<v Speaker 2>To see the profound nearness without blending the two into a muddy pantheism, and to acknowledge the utter distance without losing the intimate connection to a distant watchmaker, God, this brings us to the paradox of the veils.

340
00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:06.290
<v Speaker 2>The created order, the physical world with all its density and distraction, is traditionally understood as that which hides God.

341
00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:10.850
<v Speaker 2>The sheer density of physical matter prevents us from seeing the spiritual light.

342
00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:12.450
<v Speaker 1>That is the standard view, yes.

343
00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:19.570
<v Speaker 2>So how can the physical world be both the veil that hides him and the exact same mirror that reveals him?

344
00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.930
<v Speaker 1>The Imam answers this with the startling compressed saying.

345
00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:25.970
<v Speaker 1>The veils do not veil him.

346
00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:27.810
<v Speaker 2>The veils do not veil him.

347
00:22:27.890 --> 00:22:30.050
<v Speaker 1>Think about the profound nature of this.

348
00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:41.810
<v Speaker 1>The very things that seem to conceal the divine, the heavy density of a stone, the labyrinthine complexity of a human face, the terrifying vastness of the night sky, cannot help but disclose the Creator.

349
00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:43.010
<v Speaker 2>Because they exist.

350
00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:46.610
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the veil itself is made of the very light it attempts to obscure.

351
00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:53.410
<v Speaker 1>Once the heart is polished, the realization zons that the veil that hides him is the exact same mirror that shows him.

352
00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.370
<v Speaker 1>The physical world is not an obstacle to the divine.

353
00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:59.410
<v Speaker 1>It is the very vocabulary through which the Nerulian speaks.

354
00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:00.530
<v Speaker 2>That is incredible.

355
00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:06.930
<v Speaker 1>God has made the remembrance a polish for the hearts by which they hear after being deaf and see after being blind.

356
00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:13.410
<v Speaker 1>We have traversed the anatomy of the heart, the rust of heedlessness and the blinding light of true sight.

357
00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:18.210
<v Speaker 1>But I turn now directly to you, sitting with this knowledge in the midst of your own life.

358
00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.730
<v Speaker 1>Look closely at the landscape of your own daily routine.

359
00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:28.210
<v Speaker 1>Amidst the relentless noise, the constant movement, the sheer velocity of your obligations, what has gone totally unremembered?

360
00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:30.930
<v Speaker 1>What spaces in your day are occupied by the walking dead?

361
00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:39.490
<v Speaker 1>And what is 1 simple costless practice of turning the heart, a silent invocation while walking to your car, a moment of presence before breaking bread?

362
00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.290
<v Speaker 1>You can begin this week to start rubbing away the rust.
